EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - DAY

There is a wooden fence and an old rotary clothesline. HAL, sixties, is standing on a stepladder holding a spirit level against the fence.

TESSA, thirties, comes round the side of the house carrying a bag.

TESSA
Hello Dad. I have come up from London to help you move into the assisted living place, because you cannot really manage this house on your own any more.

HAL
Hello love. I am fine on my own, actually. I do not need any help.

TESSA
I know you feel that way, but the doctor and I both disagree.

HAL
Well. Anyway, I am busy with this fence. There is a boundary dispute with Del next door.

TESSA
A boundary dispute sounds very stressful. Please come down off that ladder.

HAL
In a minute.

INT. HAL'S KITCHEN - DAY

Tessa puts a brochure on the table.

TESSA
This is the brochure for the care home. It is very nice there. They have a jigsaw.

HAL
I do not want to look at it.

TESSA
Dad, the estate agent says we cannot even put the house on the market until the boundary dispute is resolved. So it is holding everything up. It is really important that we sort it out.

HAL
I know it is important. You have told me three times.

TESSA
Sorry. I am just stressed.

HAL
So am I.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - DAY

DEL, forties, appears at the fence.

DEL
Hello Tessa. I am Del from next door. As you know, your father's fence is nine inches onto my property and I am very unhappy about it.

TESSA
Hello Del. I am sorry about all of this. It must be very frustrating for you.

DEL
It is. My dog keeps getting through the gap and I worry he will get onto the road and be run over.

TESSA
That would be terrible.

DEL
It would. Anyway, I have instructed a surveyor.

TESSA
I understand.

Del goes back inside. Tessa stands in the garden.

INT. HAL'S FRONT ROOM - EVENING

Tessa looks at the deeds with a magnifying glass.

TESSA
According to this plan, Del is right. The boundary is nine inches on the other side of the fence.

She puts the deeds down.

TESSA (CONT'D)
I do not know what to do about that. I will speak to Dad in the morning, I suppose.

She goes to bed.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - DAY

Tessa walks around the garden looking at the hydrangea.

TESSA
Mum planted this hydrangea. I miss her very much. Everything in this garden reminds me of her.

She looks at the clothesline.

TESSA (CONT'D)
And that was her clothesline. She used to hang the washing out there every Monday.

She goes back inside.

INT. HAL'S KITCHEN - DAY

Tessa and Hal sit at the table.

TESSA
Dad, I have looked at the deeds and Del is right. The fence is in the wrong place.

HAL
I know. I put it there. Let me explain. In nineteen ninety-four your mother said to me that the corner of the garden by Del's raspberries got four hours of sun a day and everywhere else only got two, and she wanted her clothesline in the sun, because she did all the washing by hand in those days and it mattered to her more than anything. So when I put the fence up I moved it nine inches so the clothesline would be on our side. I never told her and I never told anybody, and after she died I could not bring myself to move it back, because it was the last thing I ever did for her, and I have felt guilty about it every single day for thirty years.

TESSA
That is a very moving story. I understand now.

HAL
Thank you.

TESSA
We should probably tell Del.

HAL
Probably.

EXT. DEL'S DRIVE - DAY

Del is at his door.

DEL
Hello again.

TESSA
Hello. My dad and I have been talking about the fence and we are going to sort it out soon.

DEL
That is good to hear. My surveyor is coming on Thursday anyway.

TESSA
Okay. Well. Thank you.

DEL
No problem.

She goes home.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - NIGHT

Hal stands in the garden in the dark looking at the fence.

HAL
Thirty years.

He goes back inside without touching it.

EXT. HAL'S BACK GARDEN - MORNING

The fence is still there in the morning, exactly where it was. The clothesline is empty. Del's dog barks somewhere on the other side.

Tessa comes out in her dressing gown and looks at it all for a while.

TESSA
Nothing has changed. I suppose these things take time.

She goes back inside to make the tea.

EXT. HAL'S BACK STEP - MORNING

Hal and Tessa sit on the step with cups of tea. The fence is still there.

TESSA
Dad, I want you to know that I love you and that I am not trying to take your house away from you. I am just worried about you.

HAL
I know, love. I love you too. I am sorry I have been difficult.

TESSA
You have not been difficult.

HAL
I have a bit.

TESSA
Maybe a bit.

They both laugh. Tessa puts her head on his shoulder.

HAL
I suppose we will have to sort out that fence at some point.

TESSA
At some point.

They drink their tea and look at the garden as the sun comes up over the clothesline.
